WEBVTT

00:00:00.623 --> 00:00:25.119
<v Riley>Hey there, welcome in. Today on the show we've got Mercier House in Kamloops, a bed near Royal Inland Hospital for folks travelling in for treatment, and Argentina's push to open foreign ownership of farmland and water. Then the Deep Dive asks who absorbs the loss when a global price shock hits a working farm, and whether the tools for riding it out reach the people who need them. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:38.594 --> 00:00:58.850
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that affect our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Tuesday, October 13th, 2026. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry, and we're reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:31.192
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're asking who gets paid when a ranch, a farm, or a mill runs on thin margins and the market turns. Quick weather check: out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast and around 2 degrees, with a high of 14, a low of 2, and light drizzle through the day. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 4 and partly cloudy, Williams Lake is around 2 and foggy, Quesnel is around 1 under overcast, and Redstone is around 2 and overcast. Heads up if you're driving into Williams Lake, because it's foggy at 2 degrees.

00:01:31.977 --> 00:01:38.889
<v Riley>A lot of today's news is close to home, so we'll start in Williams Lake and Quesnel before we head out into the wider world.

00:01:40.289 --> 00:02:05.913
<v Casey>Start in Williams Lake. The Station House Gallery is showing *The Ghosts of the Cariboo*, a set of oil paintings by local artist Kendal Anderson. The works depict abandoned places across the region, and they hang in the upper gallery. The Williams Lake Tribune presents the show as a way to see the Cariboo through one painter's eyes. An empty homestead is a record of someone who left, and that's a question much of the Cariboo is living with right now.

00:02:06.363 --> 00:02:12.411
<v Riley>Painting abandoned places is an odd thing to sell as a gallery visit. It's also an honest one for this region.

00:02:13.905 --> 00:02:25.801
<v Casey>In Quesnel, the Observer has published Mike Cave's answers as he seeks a council seat. We covered the format of those candidate questions on Monday. The record that matters comes later, in the votes and the minutes.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:51.682
<v Riley>A former housing co-op on Battle Street West in Kamloops has been converted into Mercier House. The building will provide temporary accommodation for people travelling from out of town to receive treatment at Royal Inland Hospital. For someone from a smaller Cariboo community, the overnight stay is often the cost that breaks the trip. A bed close to the hospital can decide whether the appointment happens at all.

00:02:52.132 --> 00:02:56.396
<v Casey>The drive is often what breaks the appointment. A bed in town takes that off the list.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:21.728
<v Casey>Argentina's president, Javier Milei, is courting investors by removing a block on foreign ownership of land and natural resources. The Guardian's report opens with 84-year-old chemistry professor Marta Sahores, one of the protesters fighting what they call foreignisation. One of their slogans is "They take our water, our soil." The rule decides who can buy the land, and who has to keep working it after the sale.

00:03:23.329 --> 00:03:43.777
<v Riley>Small Farm Canada reports that Canadian food charities face growing demand and shortages. Second Harvest redirects surplus food to non-profit organizations, and those organizations are finding less to work with. The piece describes a pattern across the country rather than a single provincial count, so treat it as a trend, not a tally.

00:03:44.227 --> 00:03:47.547
<v Casey>Redirecting surplus only works if there's surplus to redirect.

00:03:49.109 --> 00:04:04.917
<v Riley>New Atlas reports researchers have built a paper-thin membrane for the foot of a crawling microrobot. The membrane harvests electricity from the ground beneath it, so the robot doesn't have to carry heavy onboard batteries. It's a lab result, not a field test.

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:23.751
<v Casey>Fast Company reports that China's passenger car exports rose 64 percent in September compared with the same month last year, to around 919,000 vehicles. Domestic sales are sluggish, so producers are looking for buyers abroad. Anyone who ships cattle or lumber will recognize that pattern.

00:04:25.268 --> 00:04:38.740
<v Riley>The Northern Miner reports Falco Resources expects Quebec to sign off on its Horne 5 project by year end. The company has spent eight years in environmental review. This is the company's expectation, not a government decision.

00:04:40.182 --> 00:04:54.894
<v Casey>Boing Boing reports the U.S. Food and Drug Administration may let some additives into food without a safety review, as long as they aren't proven to be carcinogenic and are introduced at levels below 0.5 parts per billion. The change is reported, not finalized.

00:04:55.344 --> 00:04:59.312
<v Riley>Parts per billion is a number nobody at the grocery counter can check.

00:05:00.914 --> 00:05:18.578
<v Riley>WIRED reports astronomers have the most detailed image yet of Pa 30, the remnant of a supernova that was likely recorded from Earth in the Middle Ages. There's no working-lands angle here. Something observed close to a thousand years ago is still being photographed at higher resolution.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:29.266
<v Casey>Xbox has named a former Reddit and Facebook executive to lead Mojang, the studio behind Minecraft. The change comes as Xbox looks at broader franchising of the game.

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:57.631
<v Riley>Copernicus reports the developing El Niño pushed sea-surface temperatures in the Pacific's Niño 3.4 reference region to a record 29.70 degrees Celsius in September. Average sea temperatures outside polar waters reached a September record of 21.05 degrees. Copernicus also cautioned that those readings alone don't establish it as the strongest El Niño on record. We'll come back to that shortly.

00:05:59.203 --> 00:06:12.851
<v Casey>Al Jazeera reports a cyberattack on supermarket refrigeration systems has left shelves empty across France. The attack also caused fires, with losses running into the millions. When cooling fails, the stock is gone before anyone gets a chance to buy it.

00:06:13.301 --> 00:06:17.605
<v Riley>Cold storage is the line item nobody budgets for until it stops working.

00:06:19.226 --> 00:06:34.506
<v Riley>Business Insider profiles the founder of the Bed Scrunchie company, who says Amazon nearly destroyed the business. The company had made $4.5 million before Amazon deactivated its seller account. One of the founders still shops on Amazon every week.

00:06:36.083 --> 00:06:48.371
<v Casey>New Atlas tried ChatGPT on a $1,000 guitar effects pedal and reports on what it built. We use OpenAI's tools ourselves, so we're not casting stones. A tool's output still needs someone checking the wiring.

00:06:48.821 --> 00:06:55.933
<v Riley>The wiring is where I'd be nervous. For now, a local group doing the everyday work of food recovery and community gardens.

00:06:57.333 --> 00:07:17.461
<v Casey>The Williams Lake Food Policy Council works on local food security across the Cariboo. It supports community gardens, food recovery, and farm-to-table initiatives, so food grown here can feed people here. If you want to get involved in growing or recovering food close to home, the council is a good place to start. Check with them directly for how to connect.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:26.426
<v Riley>A group doing the unglamorous work of gardens and food recovery is what keeps a local food system standing when prices outside start moving.

00:07:26.748 --> 00:07:29.404
<v Casey>Which brings us back to the grocery shelf and the Pacific.

00:07:30.804 --> 00:07:51.804
<v Riley>The Pacific is running record warm, and the grocery forecasts reach the kitchen table. Quartz lists coffee, cocoa, rice, and sugar as staples most likely to cost more because of El Niño. CNN names coffee, chocolate, and palm oil. So when a shock hits the supply chain, who absorbs the loss, and who stays on the land to make the next crop work?

00:07:52.117 --> 00:07:53.629
<v Casey>The Cariboo doesn't grow coffee.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:08:00.897
<v Casey>But the markets that set grain and fuel prices don't stop at the border. A rancher here buys feed priced in a market he can't see.

00:08:01.453 --> 00:08:09.029
<v Riley>So this is a working-lands story, not only a shopping story. The shock lands on producers first, then buyers, then shoppers.

00:08:09.352 --> 00:08:12.184
<v Casey>Then who's holding the loss in the middle? Somebody is.

00:08:12.839 --> 00:08:26.383
<v Riley>The producer, in most cases. The coverage points to Thailand and Colombia, and both have many smallholder farms. A bad harvest means no cash for the next planting. A family then has to decide whether to stay on the land at all.

00:08:27.018 --> 00:08:31.770
<v Casey>The price chart doesn't show that. The leaving is the outcome nobody puts in the forecast.

00:08:32.459 --> 00:08:42.083
<v Riley>The tools exist. Drought-tolerant varieties, precision irrigation, better forecasting, and futures hedging can each blunt a shock like this. Access is the question.

00:08:42.601 --> 00:08:47.185
<v Casey>Futures hedging needs a broker and a credit line. Who in a smallholder village has either?

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:55.738
<v Riley>Cooperatives. Buyers. Lenders who take the risk for a whole group. The tool only has to reach the aggregators between farms and markets.

00:08:56.259 --> 00:09:00.755
<v Casey>Then the farm is a customer of the aggregator, and the aggregator sets the terms.

00:09:01.429 --> 00:09:09.661
<v Riley>Sometimes. Cooperatives have used collective buying and shared storage to get better terms for decades. That model predates any software.

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:18.234
<v Casey>Fine, I'll grant the cooperative. The coverage doesn't say whether any cooperative in the affected regions can hedge through a twelve-month shock.

00:09:18.538 --> 00:09:20.994
<v Riley>It doesn't. It also doesn't say they can't.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:29.218
<v Casey>Then consider timing. Price effects usually trail the weather by six to twelve months. That's a planning window, if you can afford to use it.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:38.960
<v Riley>Plant different, store different, and renegotiate contracts while the price is still a forecast. Producers who see the lag early have time to move.

00:09:39.593 --> 00:09:45.113
<v Casey>A farmer with a bad harvest and no savings doesn't have twelve months of cash. He has the next bill.

00:09:45.697 --> 00:09:50.937
<v Riley>Which is why the stack matters: tools, insurance, cooperative buying. No single layer covers it.

00:09:51.517 --> 00:10:08.437
<v Casey>Now the bank scenario. A Guardian report from July quotes a bank saying price shocks could reach 10 to 50 percent across core commodities. The most exposed crops, rice, palm oil, sugar, and coffee, could rise 50 to 100 percent or more, and the shock could last into 2028.

00:10:08.984 --> 00:10:15.672
<v Riley>One institution's scenario, and the bank isn't named in what we have. Treat it as a stress test, not a forecast.

00:10:16.223 --> 00:10:21.063
<v Casey>A stress test with a number attached. A shrug doesn't give anybody a budget to plan against.

00:10:21.642 --> 00:10:30.194
<v Riley>The useful part is the ranking. The crops with the thinnest supply take the biggest hit, and those are grown in the most concentrated tropical regions.

00:10:30.876 --> 00:10:35.364
<v Casey>A handful of regions grow most of the supply. When one has a bad year, everyone pays.

00:10:36.015 --> 00:10:45.495
<v Riley>Sugar is the exception. Drought in Thailand and India can cut the harvest, and it can also push the price the other way. Direction is harder to read there.

00:10:46.069 --> 00:10:51.085
<v Casey>So nobody can tell you which shelf moves first. Good to know before you buy a forecast.

00:10:51.685 --> 00:11:04.029
<v Riley>Commentary on cocoa cites a Citigroup warning that cocoa could reach $5,000 a tonne, and possibly $6,000, if West African output is hit. The timing is uncertain. The direction isn't.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:10.242
<v Casey>Chocolate gets the attention. The Cariboo's grocery bill is set by a dozen commodities nobody here grows.

00:11:10.823 --> 00:11:20.559
<v Riley>Which is the argument for putting technology into the local part of the chain, where it can still change something. Test the tech case on something measurable.

00:11:20.659 --> 00:11:40.115
<v Riley>A study in *Agronomy for Sustainable Development* looked at a rooftop greenhouse in Barcelona run by ICTA-UAB, across eleven tomato crop cycles between 2015 and 2023. The main finding is that sunlight and stage-specific nutrient timing decide success far beyond fixed recipes or miracle solutions.

00:11:40.427 --> 00:11:44.331
<v Casey>One rooftop, one crop, one university. That's a small sample for a big claim.

00:11:44.976 --> 00:11:52.360
<v Riley>Eleven cycles is a lot of data from one rooftop. Each cycle feeds the next design decision, which is what engineers want from a system.

00:11:53.047 --> 00:12:06.919
<v Casey>The number that matters is the panels. When deteriorated greenhouse panels were replaced in 2023, yields rose more than 56 percent over the year before. That's a maintenance story. The fertilizer tinkering didn't produce that jump.

00:12:07.599 --> 00:12:15.007
<v Riley>Light transmission outweighed the nutrient program. So you instrument the panels. Sensors flag degradation before yields drop.

00:12:15.322 --> 00:12:19.290
<v Casey>The study didn't test sensors. You're bolting a hypothesis onto a result.

00:12:19.616 --> 00:12:24.112
<v Riley>I'm proposing a test, and eleven cycles of records make it cheap to design.

00:12:24.766 --> 00:12:29.582
<v Casey>And who does the panel swap? When the greenhouse needs fixing, who in a small town knows how?

00:12:29.855 --> 00:12:36.319
<v Riley>Staffing is real. The answer is training people to run the system. Skipping the technology doesn't solve it.

00:12:36.833 --> 00:12:43.721
<v Casey>Training people, and then keeping them. The young person who learns the system is the one who gets offered a job in the city.

00:12:44.362 --> 00:12:57.234
<v Riley>Then pay them to stay. A system that needs a local technician is also a reason to build a local career. That reverses the leaving story. Fewer hands might run the operation, but the ones who do are skilled and local.

00:12:57.803 --> 00:13:05.499
<v Casey>Or fewer hands, and everyone else commutes out. Who pays the technician in year three, when the pilot money is gone? The study doesn't say.

00:13:06.063 --> 00:13:10.719
<v Riley>It doesn't. The budget question is the right one. It doesn't argue against the tool.

00:13:11.014 --> 00:13:13.966
<v Casey>I'm not against the tool. I'm asking who stays to run it.

00:13:14.065 --> 00:13:28.681
<v Casey>Different case. The Guardian's Argentina story describes the Milei government removing the block on foreign ownership of land and natural resources. Protesters say the country is losing its water and its soil. The government's stated aim is to attract foreign capital.

00:13:29.276 --> 00:13:36.628
<v Riley>Foreign capital is also what farms sometimes get when domestic lenders won't lend. That doesn't make it the villain by default.

00:13:37.296 --> 00:13:45.120
<v Casey>Capital that comes in as ownership sends the profit out. The farm stays, the rent leaves, and the people who work the land carry the decision.

00:13:45.767 --> 00:13:57.071
<v Riley>Ownership versus operation. A foreign owner who hires local operators and invests in irrigation is a different deal from one who strips the water. The rules decide which one you get.

00:13:57.719 --> 00:14:06.463
<v Casey>Then what are the rules on foreign ownership of farmland here? Canada sets land rules through provinces, and today's sources don't tell me what British Columbia allows.

00:14:07.032 --> 00:14:13.560
<v Riley>I don't know either. That makes it a question for the Cariboo, and a more useful one than whether the technology works.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:25.444
<v Riley>Can a Cariboo rancher stay in business when the land is valuable to someone who doesn't ranch it? Argentina's protesters are answering that with their feet, and the rule change made it urgent.

00:14:26.116 --> 00:14:37.876
<v Casey>Follow the value down the chain and you end up at the food charity line. Small Farm Canada reports growing demand and shortages across the country. When a shock hits, the bill shows up in a line at a food bank.

00:14:38.429 --> 00:14:45.229
<v Riley>Recovery is a logistics problem, and software helps there. Matching surplus to need in close to real time cuts waste.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:49.564
<v Casey>Only when there's surplus to match. Shortages mean the matching works on less.

00:14:50.155 --> 00:14:54.867
<v Riley>Which argues for building supply and keeping the matching. The two aren't rivals.

00:14:55.156 --> 00:14:56.916
<v Casey>Sure. Build the supply. Who owns it?

00:14:57.218 --> 00:15:11.282
<v Riley>The Williams Lake Food Policy Council works on community gardens, food recovery, and farm-to-table initiatives. That puts a local buyer and a local grower in the same conversation, which is the arrangement the cooperative model depends on.

00:15:11.888 --> 00:15:25.912
<v Casey>A food hub is a bet that the value can stay local, that the middle of the chain belongs to people who live here. But a shared room has thin margins. Who runs the hub in year five, when the grant is gone and the founder is tired? That's where most of these die.

00:15:26.568 --> 00:15:35.728
<v Riley>Year five is where the model has to work. A hub that charges fees for aggregation can cover operating costs without a rescue. That's the structural test.

00:15:36.048 --> 00:15:40.072
<v Casey>Maybe. I haven't seen a Cariboo hub prove it yet, and I won't pretend I have.

00:15:40.172 --> 00:15:50.932
<v Casey>The history is older than any of this. Country Life traces how sheep wealth built the parish churches of the Cotswolds. The churches are the receipts. The money went to whoever owned the flocks.

00:15:51.603 --> 00:15:57.827
<v Riley>So the wool economy rewarded owners. Does technology change that, or does it just make the owners faster?

00:15:58.173 --> 00:16:00.861
<v Casey>Faster. Not fairer. The shepherd didn't get a church.

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:06.442
<v Riley>Maybe a better fleece price in some years. I don't have data on that, so I won't claim it.

00:16:06.622 --> 00:16:07.486
<v Casey>Good. Don't claim it.

00:16:08.001 --> 00:16:14.561
<v Riley>Who owns the sensor data from a greenhouse, or from a hay operation? The rancher, the vendor, or a regional body?

00:16:14.852 --> 00:16:20.716
<v Casey>Vendor, most likely. Platform rules are often unpublished, and the logs sit wherever the vendor keeps them.

00:16:21.284 --> 00:16:36.668
<v Riley>Then it's a contract question, and contracts can be written before they're signed. A regional body could hold the data and set terms for every operator in the district. That could also keep a young technician in the Cariboo instead of commuting out.

00:16:37.334 --> 00:16:46.510
<v Casey>A regional body with a budget, a staff, and a reason to exist after the pilot ends. I'd want to see that first. And I still think automation mostly means fewer jobs.

00:16:47.099 --> 00:17:00.571
<v Riley>We disagree on whether these tools help the small operator, but the data terms are worth fighting for either way. Send us your own stories of who stayed and who left at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Have a good Tuesday.

00:17:00.868 --> 00:17:02.940
<v Casey>Mind the fog on the way into Williams Lake.